I wrote the hidden suite in `packages/core/test/hidden-categories/`, in four files: `facts.test.ts`, `rules.test.ts`, `signals.test.ts` and `registry.test.ts`. `npx tsc -p tsconfig.test.json` reports no errors, and all 113 tests fail against the current interface.

To check that my expected values are right, I wrote a throwaway reference implementation in the scratchpad, outside the repo. All 219 tests pass against it: the public suite, this suite and the existing bounty/handle tests.

I also tried some shortcuts on that reference implementation. Several pass the whole public suite but fail this one:
- `commentCount || null`, which turns a count of 0 into null;
- picking `carriedSince` by comparing the date strings rather than the times;
- keeping clauses from a failed branch inside `any`;
- upper-casing a category's `why` when it is used as the reason.

Things I left untested because the spec doesn't settle them:
- whether a `startDate` that doesn't parse falls back to `endDate`;
- which date counts as "nearer" when one of the due date and release is overdue and the plain-value and distance readings disagree;
- whether status categories other than "Done" and "done" (such as "DONE") are compared case-insensitively;
- whether category ids in the config are matched case-insensitively;
- which duplicate id is named when more than one id repeats;
- whether a rule that throws while being built makes `createClassifier` throw.

113 tests, in 4 files.

Open in the spec:
- `categories.rules.test.ts` "[boundary-11] any evaluates every child even after one has matched": it asserts each child's `test` runs exactly once. The spec only says `any` calls the test of both children, not how many times.
- `categories.rules.test.ts` "[boundary-12] inside any, an unmatched signal is not described while a matched sibling is": it asserts the matched sibling's `describe` runs exactly once. The spec only says `describe` runs after `test` matched.
- `categories.registry.test.ts` "[boundary-18] each resolve returns fresh objects": it asserts the resolved `thresholds` is a different object from the frozen `CATEGORIES[0].defaults`. The spec scenario only requires each resolved category to be a new object and the registry to stay unchanged; it doesn't say whether the frozen defaults object may be reused as `thresholds`.
